The tests use `url: 'https://example.test/8pack'` for the 8-pack, so my `COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[url]` lookup won't match the test URL. I should key the quote lookup on the URL but allow tests to pass the real URL, or better: detect the offer by pack count/grams rather than URL. The parser knows classification: packCount 8, totalGrams 2000, variant classico. But a real 2 kg multipack from another vendor? The checkout quote is CoffeeMan-specific; the parser is CoffeeMan-only, so keying on totalGrams === 2000 && packCount === 8 is safe.

Actually keying on url is more precise but makes tests use the real URL. I could change the tests to pass the real URL. But `parseCoffeemanProduct(html, { url: 'https://example.test/8pack' })` is used to test itemUrl passthrough. Simplest robust approach: look up by both URL and shape:

```js
const checkoutQuote = COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[url]
  ?? (classification.packCount === 8 && classification.totalGrams === 2000 ? COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[EIGHT_PACK_URL] : null);
```

Hmm, that's a bit hacky. Alternatively, use the observation shape in the tests by passing the real URL. But the test asserts `itemUrl === 'https://example.test/8pack'`, which is nice for generic parsing.

I think keying on shape is acceptable and documented: the adapter tracks exactly these three listings; the 2 kg 8-pack offer is the only cart the quote applies to. But wait — the same parser is used for all CoffeeMan listings; if CoffeeMan later carries another 8×250 g line... it wouldn't match the product classification anyway (only Crema e Gusto is tracked). Fine.

Actually cleaner: the quote is attached by offer, and the `observe()` loop knows `offer.id`. I can pass `offerId` into the parser. Let me do that: `parseCoffeemanProduct(html, { now, url, productId, offerId: offer.id })`, and in the parser `const checkoutQuote = offerId ? COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[offerId] : COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[EIGHT_PACK_URL]`? Hmm, tests call without offerId.

Simplest: key by shape. Let me do it and document.

Actually wait: `COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES` keyed by URL is used in the shipping note and evidence. I'll keep the key by URL for documentation but look up with a helper:

```js
function checkoutQuoteFor({ url, classification }) {
  if (COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[url]) return COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[url];
  // The 8 × 250 g = 2 kg listing is the only cart the researched quote was observed on.
  if (classification.packCount === 8 && classification.totalGrams === 2000) return COFFEEMAN_CHECKOUT_QUOTES[EIGHT_PACK_URL];
  return null;
}
```

