`waitForHome` waits only for the card, not for the footer status. The footer fetch is a separate async request; on a slow network (production from the runner) it can still be pending when the evaluate runs. That's a QA race — a test robustness defect. Fix: make `waitForHome` also wait for the footer status to resolve (e.g. `#footer-status` text not matching "Waiting for the latest observation time…" or `last checked`). That's a small, meaningful QA fix ("adds a meaningful rendered check" style). Alternatively, in the check itself, wait for the footer.

Better: update `waitForHome` to wait until the footer no longer shows the placeholder, with a bounded timeout; but careful: on a fresh deployment with no observations, the footer shows "No offer observation is stored yet..." which is also a resolved state. So wait for `!text.includes('Waiting for the latest observation time')`.

This is a genuine robustness fix for the production QA. Let me implement it:

```js
async function waitForHome(wd) {
  await waitFor(async () => evaluate(wd, `!!document.querySelector('#catalog-list .product-card')`), 20_000, 'home catalog render');
